The whole bug class fits in one line. This renders a user-supplied display name into a greeting:

```js
// VULNERABLE — assigns an untrusted string into a markup-parsing sink
const name = await fetchProfileName(userId);   // whatever the user typed
greeting.innerHTML = 'Welcome back, ' + name;
```

If a user sets their display name to `<script>alert(1)</script>`, that `alert` is what everybody viewing the profile gets. `alert(1)` is the harmless canary researchers use to prove execution; what a real attacker substitutes is arbitrary.

The fix is a one-character-per-line change of **sink**:

```js
// SAFE — textContent never parses markup, only sets text
greeting.textContent = 'Welcome back, ' + name;
```

`textContent` inserts the same characters as *text*, so `<` shows up as a literal less-than sign on screen. The rule that generalises: reach for a markup-parsing sink only when rendering markup is genuinely the feature, and then sanitize deliberately.

A pattern that ships constantly — restoring a tab from the URL fragment:

```js
// VULNERABLE: location.hash is a source, innerHTML is a sink
const tab = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
header.innerHTML = 'Section: ' + tab;   // markup-parsing sink
```

Request `/dashboard#billing` and it behaves. Put markup after the `#` instead of a section name and it executes — and because fragments are stripped by the browser before the request goes out, your server access logs show a plain `GET /dashboard`.

The fix is the same shape as before, plus validating that the value is one you recognise:

```js
const ALLOWED = new Set(['overview', 'billing', 'team']);
const raw = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
const tab = ALLOWED.has(raw) ? raw : 'overview';   // allowlist, not blocklist
header.textContent = 'Section: ' + tab;
```

Two defenses stacked: an **allowlist** of expected values, and a **non-parsing sink**. Either alone would have held; together they survive one of them being edited later by someone who didn't know why it was there.
